--TEST--
`Core\Request::body()` answers the same octets on every call, and the `post()` that follows reads its form out of what that reading held `rule:http-server/buffering-readers-share-the-body-and-streaming-readers-consume-it`
--POST--
title=Q3+report
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A buffering reader keeps what it read, so a second call is not a second
// reading of the wire: it is a copy out of the hold. A member that pulled
// again would print an empty second line here -- "the peer sent nothing",
// about octets this program has already been handed -- and one that refused
// its own second call would print a `LogicError` instead of either.
echo Core\Request::body(), "\n";
echo Core\Request::body(), "\n";
// The reader that follows is a different member and the rule is the same one:
// `post` parses the octets `body` held, where parsing the wire would find it
// drained by then and answer a form with no fields in it.
echo Core\Request::post("title") ?? "", "\n";
// And the hold belongs to the request rather than to the member that filled
// it, so the octets are still what `body` answers once a form has been parsed
// out of them.
echo Core\Request::body(), "\n";
--EXPECT--
title=Q3+report
title=Q3+report
Q3 report
title=Q3+report
